207:Each run seed derives separate streams. The stream seed is the first 8 bytes of the SHA-256 of the UTF-8 text `<run seed>|<label>`, read as a big-endian unsigned 64-bit integer. Labels: `tape`, `choice`, `reservoir`, `replay`, `shuffle`, `filler`, `features`. Each stream is `xoshiro256**` with its 256-bit state filled by four SplitMix64 outputs from the stream seed (the standard constructions). Uniform integer in 0 to m−1: draw 64-bit outputs, accept one only if it is below 2^64 − (2^64 mod m), and return it mod m. Uniform real in [0, 1): the top 53 bits divided by 2^53. Normal (random projection only): each normal value takes two uniform reals u1 then u2, in that order (u1 replaced by 2^−53 if it is 0), and returns √(−2 ln u1) · cos(2π u2); the sine partner is discarded, so no value is reused. The projection fills W row by row, one normal per entry, column by column within a row. Stage 0 checks the first eight normals of the `features` stream for each registered run seed against reference values computed by an independent implementation (Python standard library: `hashlib`, and a separate `xoshiro256**` and Box–Muller), committed to the protocol file before any run. A value matches when it differs from its reference by at most 10⁻¹² in absolute terms; any mismatch stops the run. Permutations: Fisher–Yates driven by the uniform-integer rule. Run seeds are fixed before any run: stage 1 uses the original six seeds 4101–4106 (development data, as the original spec states); stage 2 uses 20261201, 20261202 and 20261203. One stream never feeds another's decisions, so, for example, admitting a lesson cannot change which entry is replayed next except through the diary's contents.
349:**Matched control input.** The matched tape has four fields only: the delivered stimulus, the external teaching input, the event kind (lesson or replay) and the order. Every row carries a pulse: the lesson rule already excludes trials without one, and the tape comes from the full diary fly, whose replays always carry its own pulse, so no pulse-free row can appear. Replay logs (dopamine factors, update sizes, the chosen entry) are fly-internal and are not on the tape. Each control arm with a diary receives, for the fly it is compared with, the tape up to the exact receipt cutoff of that fly's selected snapshot, including whether that snapshot's last replay was applied; no event after the cutoff is given. Each control then uses the tape as follows: the final-diary lookup reads that fly's frozen diary at the cutoff; the full-history lookup has one entry per original lesson receipt (replays are never stored as cases); the simple learners apply every tape event as an update. The protocol's missing-idea and filler conditions are kept as the protocol defines them, so a control that lacks an idea never receives the omitted lessons. Fly arms choose for themselves, so they see different lessons; for the central attribution test, the learner arms are matched to the evaluated full fly's tape. The auditor checks byte equality of the matched tape for each comparison, separately from each arm's own update records, and checks the diary bytes each lookup consumes against the selected checkpoint's manifest hash (section 4.7), so a later diary cannot pass.
